.TH VALLUM 8 "2026-10-19" "vallum 0.1.4" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
vallum \- hybrid post-quantum layer-3 tunnel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vallum
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vallum
runs a layer-3 tunnel with a tri-hybrid KEM handshake and an explicit,
mutually authenticated key confirmation. It is configured by a TOML file; the
daemon owns the tunnel, the TUN interface and the UDP socket, and nothing else.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run --config FILE
Run the tunnel. Requires CAP_NET_ADMIN for the interface and socket.
.TP
.B keygen --output FILE [--suite ID] [--signing]
Generate a private key and print the peer public line.
.TP
.B pubkey --private-key FILE
Print the public line for an existing key.
.TP
.B genpsk
Print a fresh 32-byte preshared key as 64 hex characters.
.TP
.B show [--config FILE | --control SOCKET] [--dump] [--json] [--watch] [--field NAME]
Query a running daemon's control socket. `--json` is secret-free; `--watch`
refreshes until Ctrl-C.
.TP
.B setconf FILE
Replace every peer with the config's peers.
.TP
.B addconf FILE
Add or update the config's peers.
.TP
.B syncconf FILE
Apply only the differences, preserving unchanged sessions.
.TP
.B showconf [--config FILE | --control SOCKET] [--include-secrets]
Export a config; secrets are omitted unless explicitly requested.
.TP
.B bench [--suite ID] [--bytes N] [--iterations N]
Measure the data plane's seal/open cost.
.TP
.B doctor [--config FILE | --control SOCKET] [--probe-mtu HOST] [--json]
Check the platform, config, control socket, forwarding, the firewall port, the
unit state, and the config's permissions. `--probe-mtu` is the one active
check: it bisects the path to HOST with `ping -M do` and compares the result
with `outer_mtu`.
.TP
.B completions SHELL
Print a completion script for bash, zsh, fish, or PowerShell.
.TP
.B server init [--config FILE] [--endpoint HOST:PORT] [--address CIDR] [--listen ADDR:PORT]
.PP
Prepare this host as a tunnel server: generate or reuse the key, write the
config, enable IP forwarding, open the UDP port, install the masquerade rule,
and install and start the systemd units. Idempotent. `--dry-run` prints the
plan; `--no-nat`, `--no-firewall`, `--no-forward`, `--no-host`, and
`--no-service` disable individual steps; `--json` is the machine interface.
.TP
.B peer add NAME (--public-key LINE | --public-key-file FILE | --bundle-out FILE) [--address CIDR] [--allowed-ips LIST] [--endpoint HOST:PORT] [--keepalive SECS] [--psk] [--client-config-out FILE] [--forget-key] [--json]
.PP
Enrol a peer. Allocates an address from the interface's subnets unless one is
given, refuses an overlapping prefix, writes the config without reformatting it,
and applies it to a running daemon. `--public-key` keeps the private key on the
device; `--bundle-out` generates it here and writes a client bundle.
.TP
.B peer list | show NAME | remove NAME [--json]
Manage the configured peers. `list` prints a fingerprint rather than the 4.7 KB
public line; `show --full` prints the line.
.TP
.B peer rotate NAME (--public-key LINE | --public-key-file FILE | --bundle-out FILE | --identity FILE) [--endpoint HOST:PORT] [--no-apply] [--json]
.PP
Install a new identity for a peer and keep the old one as
.BR previous_public_key .
The peer accepts both until
.BR "peer finalize" ,
so the server can rotate before the device has the new key. A rotation is only
zero-downtime when the rotating peer is the responder: an initiator dials with
its current key.
.TP
.B peer finalize NAME [--no-apply] [--json]
Retire the previous identity a rotation kept. The old key is refused afterwards.
.TP
.B metrics [--config FILE | --control SOCKET] [--prefix PREFIX]
Print the daemon's state as Prometheus text exposition. One
.B get=1
is one scrape; there is no listener. Labels carry the interface, the peer name
and its fingerprint, never the public line.
.TP
.B identity issue --ca-key FILE (--peer-public LINE | --peer-public-file FILE) --label NAME --days N [--output FILE] [--json]
Issue an identity document signed by a CA key that carries a signing half. The
document binds the peer's KEM key and signing key to a label and a validity
window.
.TP
.B identity self-sign --key FILE --label NAME --days N [--output FILE] [--json]
Issue a self-signed document. It is a key binding with a window, not an
authorisation: with
.B interface.identity_issuer
set, a self-signed document is refused.
.TP
.B identity verify [--issuer FILE | --issuer-public LINE] [--suite ID] --document FILE [--json]
Check a document's signature and window. Without an issuer, a self-signed
document is checked against its own key.
.TP
.B identity show [--suite ID] --document FILE [--json]
Print a document's label, key sizes, window and fingerprint without verifying it.
.TP
.B import-bundle FILE [--output-dir DIR] [--force] [--install]
Import a client `.vallum-bundle` as a config and a 0600 key file. `--install`
writes to `/etc/vallum`, installs the units, and starts the tunnel.
.TP
.B tui [--config FILE | --control SOCKET | --config-dir DIR] [--snapshot]
Open the terminal operations console. `--snapshot` renders one frame headlessly.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vallum/*.toml
Configuration files.
.TP
.I /run/vallum/*.sock
Control sockets, mode 0600.
.SH SECURITY
The private key is refused if its file is group- or world-readable, unless
.B --allow-insecure-key-permissions
is given. The control socket is same-user only. The daemon runs no shell and
writes no routes; use
.BR vallum-quick (8)
for host-level work. The TUI spawns `systemctl`, `journalctl`, `vallum-quick`,
and `$EDITOR` with argument arrays; it does not open a shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vallum-quick (8),
.BR vallum.toml (5)
